"Image-Based Abuse: What To Know and Do | Consumer Advice (https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/image-based-abuse-what-know-and-do)\nciteturn214view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/image-based-abuse-what-know-and-do\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 80\nL0: cite0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com cite1†Skip to main content L1: \nL2: Did someone share, or threaten to share, an intimate photo or video of you without your permission? Did this happen to your child? If that’s happened to you or someone you know, here’s information to help you decide what to do.\nL3: \nL4:   * cite2†What Is Image-Based Abuse? L5:   * cite3†What To Do If Someone Posts Your Intimate Images Without Your Consent L6:   * cite4†Where To Find Other Help L7: ## What Is Image-Based Abuse?\nL8: \nL9: Image-based abuse is when someone creates, shares, or threatens to share, an intimate photo or video of a person without their permission, otherwise known as a nonconsensual intimate image. This involves sexually explicit photos or videos of you shared without your consent. These intimate images may be real, digitally altered, or deepfakes created with artificial intelligence.\nL10: Harmful digital exploitation like image-based abuse or deepfakes can happen to adults and minors. The perpetrator may be motivated by anger, revenge, control, or financial extortion. For example, someone involved in a romantic relationship may want to coerce their partner to stay in the relationship. Or an ex-partner may want to retaliate against the person who ended the relationship.\nL11: In other cases, the perpetrator might threaten to share someone’s intimate images to blackmail them to send money or more intimate content.\nL12: ## What To Do If Someone Posts Your Intimate Images Without Your Consent\nL13: \nL14: Having someone share an intimate image of you online without your consent can be stressful and overwhelming. These images can spread quickly and cause real and lasting harm. If this happens to you, the first question on your mind might be how you can get it taken down.\nL15: The Take It Down Act says certain platforms — like social media, messaging, image or video sharing apps and sites — have to give you a way to ask for the image to be taken down. And they must remove the image, and known identical copies, within 48 hours of your request. The law applies to real images, digitally altered images, and deepfakes created with artificial intelligence.\nL16: ### Ask the platform to take it down\nL17: \nL18: Only platforms can take down intimate photos and videos shared without your consent. If this happens to you, ask the platform to take it down. They have 48 hours to remove it and identical copies.\nL19: > If the platform doesn’t take down the image or video within 48 hours, report it to the FTC at cite5†TakeItDown.ftc.gov†takeitdown.ftc.gov . You can also report a platform if it doesn’t have a process for you to request removal of the intimate image or if its removal request process is broken.\nL20: #### How to ask a platform to take down a nonconsensual intimate image\nL21: \nL22: The way to report an intimate photo or video may vary by platform and may depend on whether you have an account. Here's what to look for:\nL23:   * If you have an account with the platform, log in, find the intimate image, and look for an option on or near the content to report it. Often, you'll see three dots in the upper right corner of the image.\nL24:   * If you don't have an account with the platform, search the platform's help center to find out how to report an intimate image shared without your permission. (If the platform requires you to create an account to request the removal of an intimate image, report the platform to the FTC at cite5†TakeItDown.ftc.gov†takeitdown.ftc.gov .)\nL25: Need help learning how to request removal from a platform?\nL26: Adults  | Minors\nL27: Use cite6†the directory of social media contacts†stopncii.org from StopNCII.org, a free global tool operated by the non-profit Revenge Porn Helpline.  | The National Center for Missing and Exploited Children (NCMEC) has cite7†instructions for reporting content to platforms†www.missingkids.org .\nL28:    | Or use NCMEC's cite8†CyberTipline†www.missingkids.org to report the image.\nL29: ### Limit the spread online\nL30: \nL31: Take steps to limit the intimate image from spreading online.\nL32: \nL33: Adults  | Minors\nL34: If you have a copy of the image, use cite9†StopNCII.org†stopncii.org , a free global tool operated by the non-profit Revenge Porn Helpline.  | If you have a copy of the image, use cite10†NCMEC's Take It Down†takeitdown.ncmec.org service.\nL35:    | To report the image, use NCMEC's cite8†CyberTipline†www.missingkids.org .\nL36: ### Report the perpetrator\nL37: \nL38: Report perpetrators to local criminal law enforcement and the Federal Bureau of Investigation:\nL39: \nL40:   * Online at cite11†tips.fbi.gov†tips.fbi.gov .\nL41:   * Call 1-800-CALL-FBI.\nL42:   * Visit an cite12†FBI field office†www.fbi.gov .\nL43: ### What to do if someone is blackmailing you\nL44: \nL45: If someone threatens to share an intimate image of you unless you follow their demands, here’s what to do:\nL46:   * Report it to criminal law enforcement. This is a serious crime. You can report it to your local police, as well as the FBI (cite13†tips.fbi.gov†tips.fbi.gov or call 1-800-CALL-FBI). If it involves a minor, you also can report it through NCMEC’s cite14†CyberTipline†www.missingkids.org . NCMEC has trained analysts that will process the report and make it available to the appropriate law enforcement agency.\nL47:   * Ignore their demands. Don’t pay them. Don’t give them access to your financial accounts. Don’t send them any images. Don’t give them access to your devices.\nL48:   * Cut off all contact. Block the person. Ignore their messages. Stop communicating with them in any way.\nL49:   * Speak with someone you trust. Talk to a trusted friend, family member, or counselor and tell them what’s going on.\nL50: ## Where To Find Other Help\nL51: \nL52: If you’re dealing with image-based abuse, these organizations can provide more support:\nL53: \nL54: The National Domestic Violence Hotline\nL55: \nL56:   * Call 1-800-799-SAFE (7233)\nL57:   * Chat live at cite15†thehotline.org/†www.thehotline.org L58:   * Text “START” to 88788\nL59: \nL60: The National Center for Missing and Exploited Children\nL61: \nL62:   * Call 1-800-THE-LOST (1-800-843-5678)\nL63:   * Visit cite16†missingkids.org/gethelpnow/support†www.missingkids.org L64:   * Email TakeItDown@ncmec.org\nL65: \nL66: Search Terms\nL67: \nL68: cite17†consumer privacy L69: Topics\nL70: \nL71: cite18†Identity Theft and Online Security L72: \nL73: cite19†Online Privacy and Security L74: \nL75: cite20†Protecting Kids Online L76: \nL77: May 2026\nL78: \nL79: cite21†Return to top --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFTC Begins Enforcing the TAKE IT DOWN Act | Federal Trade Commission (https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2026/05/ftc-begins-enforcing-take-it-down-act)\nciteturn214view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2026/05/ftc-begins-enforcing-take-it-down-act\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 34\nL0: cite0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com cite1†Skip to main content L1: \nL2: Tags:\nL3: \nL4:   * cite2†Consumer Protection L5:   * cite3†Bureau of Consumer Protection L6:   * cite4†Privacy and Security L7:   * cite5†Children's Privacy L8:   * cite6†Consumer Privacy L9:   * cite7†Social Media L10: \nL11: The Federal Trade Commission today began enforcing the TAKE IT DOWN Act (TIDA), a law requiring platforms, at the request of victims, to remove intimate photos or videos shared online without victims’ consent.\nL12: As part of its enforcement role, the FTC has launched cite8†TakeItDown.ftc.gov†takeitdown.ftc.gov , a website allowing victims and survivors to submit complaints about platforms that have failed to act on valid requests for the removal of nonconsensual intimate images. The website also accepts complaints about platforms that have failed to create a process for people to request removal of these images.\nL13: “Thanks to First Lady Melania Trump’s dedication, the public, especially children, will have recourse against digital exploitation and extortion,” said FTC Chairman Andrew N. Ferguson. “In the age of AI, anyone can be targeted, and that becomes even more appalling if children are involved. The TAKE IT DOWN Act empowers families and provides the FTC with an effective tool to protect minors against this form of abuse.”\nL14: The FTC enforces Section 3 of the TAKE IT DOWN Act, which set a May 19, 2026, deadline for covered platforms to give people a way to request the removal of intimate photos or videos shared online without their consent and to remove those intimate images, and known identical copies, within 48 hours of a valid request. The FTC has cite9†published new guidance to help consumers†consumer.ftc.gov in the event that nonconsensual intimate images of them are posted online.\nL15: The FTC also issued cite10†guidance for businesses about how to ensure they are complying with the law.\nL16: Underscoring the FTC’s commitment, Chairman Ferguson cite11†sent letters last week to major platforms—Alphabet, Amazon, Apple, Automattic, Bumble, Discord, Match Group, Meta, Microsoft, Pinterest, Reddit, SmugMug, Snapchat, TikTok and X—reminding them of businesses’ obligation to comply fully with TIDA no later than May 19, 2026.\nL17: The Federal Trade Commission works to promote competition and cite12†protect and educate consumers . The FTC will never demand money, make threats, tell you to transfer money, or promise you a prize. Learn more about consumer topics at cite13†consumer.ftc.gov†consumer.ftc.gov , or report fraud, scams, and bad business practices at cite14†ReportFraud.ftc.gov†reportfraud.ftc.gov .\nL18: Follow the cite15†FTC on social media , read cite16†consumer alerts†consumer.ftc.gov and the cite17†business blog , and cite18†sign up to get the latest FTC news and alerts .\nL19: ## Press Release Reference\nL20: \nL21: cite11†FTC Chairman Ferguson Advises Companies to Comply with the Take It Down Act L22: \nL23: ## Contact Information\nL24: \nL25: ### Media Contact\nL26: \nL27: Office of Public Affairs (opa@ftc.gov)\nL28: \nL29: Office of Public Affairs\nL30: \nL31: 202-326-2180\nL32: \nL33: cite19†Return to top --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMan gets 15 years for extorting women with AI-generated porn videos (https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/man-gets-15-years-in-prison-for-cyberstalking-and-sextortion/)\nciteturn214view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/man-gets-15-years-in-prison-for-cyberstalking-and-sextortion/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 108\nL0: [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input: Search Site]\nL1: \nL2: [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: # Man gets 15 years for extorting women with AI-generated porn videos\nL5: \nL6: By\nL7: ###### cite0†Sergiu Gatlan L8: \nL9:   * September 9, 2026\nL10:   * 04:44 AM\nL11:   * cite1†0 L12: \nL13: cite2†Image: Cyberstalking†www.bleepstatic.com L14: \nL15: An Ohio man was sentenced to 15 years in prison for multiple cybercrimes, including sextortion and cyberstalking of numerous victims via phone calls, voicemails, text messages, and online posts.\nL16: When he was arrested on federal charges on June 23, 2025, 37-year-old James Strahler II cite3†was also charged†storage.courtlistener.com with anonymous telecommunications harassment and child pornography production and distribution.\nL17: \nL18: According to court documents, Strahler used more than 100 AI web-based models and more than 24 AI platforms to generate sexually explicit videos and images of his victims.\nL19: \"From December 2024 until June 2025, Strahler sent harassing messages to at least six adult female victims. These messages included nude images of the victims, both real and AI-generated,\" the Justice Department cite4†said on Tuesday†www.justice.gov .\nL20: \"Strahler created more than 700 images of both real victims and animated persons and posted them to a website dedicated to child sexual abuse. An additional 2,400 images and videos on his phone were flagged as depicting nudity, morphed child sexual abuse material or violence.\"\nL21: He shared AI-generated pornographic videos with his victims' co-workers and demanded nude photos from the mothers of multiple women he was harassing, threatening to leak the forged obscene content of their daughters if they didn't comply.\nL22: \nL23: Strahler also left his victims voicemails with rape threats that mentioned their home addresses and posted online child sexual abuse material he had generated using AI.\nL24: Strahler pleaded guilty in April to cyberstalking, producing obscene visual representations of child sexual abuse, and publication of digital forgeries charges, and was the first defendant to be convicted of violating the new Take It Down Act.\nL25: \nL26: The cite5†Take It Down Act†www.govinfo.gov was enacted in 2025 to prohibit online publication of explicit content and AI forgeries without consent (also known as \"revenge porn\").\nL27: More details on how to protect yourself from cyberstalking attempts and take down images and videos shared online without consent are available on the FTC's cite6†Take It Down†takeitdown.ftc.gov platform.\nL28: \nL29: In March, 22-year-old Jamarcus Mosley also cite7†pleaded guilty to cyberstalking, extortion, and computer fraud charges after hacking into the social media accounts of hundreds of young women.\nL30: More recently, in July, 26-year-old Kyle Svara was cite8†sentenced to 76 months in prison after pleading guilty to cite9†hacking nearly 600 women's Snapchat accounts to steal private nude photos.\nL31: ## cite10†Build your security blueprint for AI-powered attacks†hubs.li L32: \nL33: Join Mikko Hyppönen and security leaders from the NFL, CHANEL, and Atlassian for a two-hour digital summit on what AI-speed attacks change, what defenders should stop doing, and how to validate, decide, fix, and re-validate at machine speed.\nL34: \nL35: cite10†Save your seat†hubs.li L36: \nL37:   * cite11†AI L38:   * cite12†Cyberstalking L39:   * cite13†Ohio L40:   * cite14†Sextortion L41:   * cite15†USA L42: \nL43:   *   *   *   *   * ##### cite0†Sergiu Gatlan sergiu@bleepingcomputer.com\nL44: Sergiu is a news reporter who has covered the latest cybersecurity and technology developments for over a decade. Email or Twitter DMs for tips.\nL45: \nL46:   * cite16†Previous Article L47:   * cite17†Next Article L48: ##### Post a Comment cite18†Community Rules L49: ###### You need to login in order to post a comment\nL50: \nL51: [Input]\nL52: \nL53: Not a member yet? cite19†Register Now L54: \nL55:   Upcoming Webinar\nL56: \nL57: Popular Stories\nL58: \nL59:   * cite20†Microsoft September 2026 Patch Tuesday fixes 966 flaws, 2 zero-days L60:   * cite21†BigBear Microsoft 365 phishing service bypassed MFA at 258 organizations L61:   * cite16†New Microsoft Defender 'ShieldCrash' zero-day grants SYSTEM access L62: \nL63: S ponsor Posts\nL64:   * cite22†Overdue a password health-check? Audit your Active Directory for free†specopssoft.com L65:   * cite23†See how attackers exploit passkey enrollment, and why hardware-bound biometrics raise enterprise identity assurance.†www.tokencore.com L66:   * cite24†Find your gaps before an auditor does. Check your EU CRA readiness in 5 questions. †go.activestate.com L67:   * cite25†EtherHiding Malware on macOS: How Attackers Hide C2 on the Blockchain†www.prophetsecurity.ai L68: \nL69:   Upcoming Webinar\nL70: ##### Follow us:\nL71: \nL72: ##### Main Sections\nL73: \nL74:   * cite26†News L75:   * cite27†Webinars L76:   * cite28†VPN Buyer Guides L77:   * cite29†SysAdmin Software Guides L78:   * cite30†Downloads L79:   * cite31†Virus Removal Guides L80:   * cite32†Tutorials L81:   * cite33†Startup Database L82:   * cite34†Uninstall Database L83:   * cite35†Glossary L84: \nL85: ##### Community\nL86: \nL87:   * cite36†Forums L88:   * cite37†Forum Rules L89:   * cite38†Chat L90: \nL91: ##### Useful Resources\nL92: \nL93:   * cite39†Welcome Guide L94:   * cite40†Sitemap L95: ##### Company\nL96: \nL97:   * cite41†About BleepingComputer L98:   * cite42†Contact Us L99:   * cite43†Send us a Tip! L100:   * cite44†Advertising L101:   * cite45†Write for BleepingComputer L102:   * cite46†Social & Feeds L103:   * cite47†Changelog L104: \nL105: cite48†Terms of Use - cite49†Privacy Policy - cite50†Ethics Statement - cite51†Affiliate Disclosure L106: \nL107: Copyright @ 2003 - 2026 cite26†Bleeping Computer^{®} LLC - All Rights Reserved\n"
